00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.480
Hello, everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel, and my name is Paul today's topic is the Scots Language

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.080
Scots is a language spoken in Scotland; one of the constituent countries of the United Kingdom. Right off the bat

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.780
It's important to point out that there are essentially three different languages in Scotland: Scottish English,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:31.240
Scots, and Gaelic. Gaelic is a Celtic language that is only distantly related to English and Scots as an Indo-European language.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.760
Scots and Scottish English on the other hand are closely related to each other, though they're not the same thing

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.040
Scottish English is for the most part standard English

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.230
But spoken with a Scottish accent. Scots, on the other hand is

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:51.119
distinct, having diverged from a dialect of Middle English and having features that differ from English. Some people consider Scots to be a separate language

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.550
While others consider it to be a historical dialect of English just like the historical dialects found in England

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:01.709
But we'll get into that a little bit later. According to the 2011 Scottish census, just over

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.860
1.5 million people speak Scots; around 30% of the Scottish population.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.040
History

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.980
The roots of scots lie in Northumbrian Old English; one of several Old English dialects

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:19.229
Northumbrian Old English was being spoken by the seventh century in the Anglo-Saxon, Kingdom of Northumbria

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:27.320
which was located in Northeastern England and part of the Scottish lowlands. The Kingdom of Scotland was founded in the year 843 with the

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:34.500
unification of the Pictish people and Gaelic speaking Scots, but English-speaking northumbrian lands were not yet part of the kingdom

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.820
when the northern part of Northumbria finally became part of Scotland in the 11th century,

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:45.869
Gaelic became the prestige language of the area, but [English] remained the most widely spoken language

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:49.229
the variety of English spoken there may have been

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:57.640
Significantly influenced by norse because the southern part of Northumbria along with much of Northern and Eastern England had been under Danish control,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.660
and its English dialects had been influenced by Norse,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:08.920
and Scandinavian settlers were also present beyond the border of that area, as well. In the 12th and 13th centuries during the Middle English period,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.460
the borough system of administrative division

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.960
led to the spread of English further to Scotland.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:21.959
Borås were autonomous urban communities where traders merchants and craftsmen could live and do [business] in exchange for paying taxes on their earnings

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:28.769
The boroughs were ruled by Nobles who answered to the king they attracted economic migrants from other parts of scotland including

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.239
Gallic speaking areas from England especially from areas that had been under danish

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:40.410
Control where norse influence dialects were spoken and from farther away places like Flanders [freesias] and scandinavia

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.700
English became the common language of the borough's but the multilingual environment

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.060
created by the

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:53.100
economic migrants had an influence on the language so as English became more widespread in scotland because of the borough's it also, began to

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:58.379
diverge from Northumbrian middle English other influences were latin because of its role as the

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.610
ecclesiastical language and the language of laws and record-keeping and [norman-French]

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.800
because of the influence of King David the first who had close ties with Norman England the

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:13.260
Divergent variety of Middle English that grew out of these influences is now referred to as early [scot]

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.739
But at the time it continued to be referred to as English until the end of the 15th [century] in the 14th and [15th] centuries

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:26.189
English replaced garlic in much [of] the Lowlands as well as replacing French as the administrative language as well as

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.769
replacing latin as the language of records and law

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:35.100
Another thing that happened in the 15th century was that the orkney islands and the Shetland Islands came under Scottish rule

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:42.450
Scandinavian settlers had been living on these islands since the [ninth] century and spoke a language called [norn] that derived from old norse

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:48.059
But Scott speaking settlers began to move there and the prestige language became scots

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:55.320
From this contact new dialects of scots would develop with more norse influence than other forms of scots these dialects are

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:58.739
Shetland scots and Arcadian scots or orkney

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.350
By this point scots had become fairly

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:08.729
Distinct from English as spoken in England one thing that set them apart was that the Southern English dialect of London was becoming the standard

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:15.269
In England whereas scots had more in common with the Northern dialects another thing that set them apart was the great vowel shift

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.029
the Great vowel shift took place between the years

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:25.080
1350 and 1600 more or less and caused vowels of English to be pronounced differently this also affected scots

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.079
Although the specific chain were different and some vowels that changed in English remained

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.520
Unchanged in scots for example in Middle English the letters o you

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:40.490
Represented the sound boom in Southern English and thus in Standard English this sound shifted to ow

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:48.709
But in scots it remained unchanged so today in standard English you say mouse, but in scots you say moose, no

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:55.459
[alumnus] and a brown cow in English is a broom coup in scots who knew [bru] [gu]

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.620
The great vowel shift took place at about the same time that English orthography was becoming

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.910
Standardized so that English spelling often reflected earlier Middle English pronunciation

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:11.239
and it still does today scots on the other hand was written more freely to reflect the way it was really pronounced an

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.800
extensive body of scots literature was written in the 15th and 16th centuries

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.640
But towards the end of that time period As english was growing in prestige

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.930
Scots was increasingly being written using a combination of scots and English

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:29.660
spelling's when King James six of scotland became the unified Monarch of England and Ireland in

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:36.140
1603 and began to rule from London the influence of English continued to grow with the Scottish upper classes

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:42.529
Adapting and Anglo sizing their speech and writing. This is what ultimately led to the development of Scottish Standard English

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.799
Scots was still widely spoken by the common people

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:52.280
[but] with the full union of England and scotland in 1707 it became even more discouraged and frowned upon

00:05:53.010 --> 00:06:00.559
But the marginalization of scots caused a rebound later that century with the scots literary revival, led by poets such as Allan ramsay

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:08.390
Later Robert burns widely considered the National Poet of scotland wrote in a combination of vernacular scots and Scottish English

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:16.339
Scots language literature declined once again in the nineteenth century, but experienced revivals again in the 20th century

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:22.039
Poet Hugh macdiarmid attempted to create a standard form of literary scots based on a

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.609
